window.STUDY_DAYS.push({"blocks":[{"label":"Warm-up","steps":["Re-read the Domain 4 task statements in the exam guide (PDF) and highlight every verb about monitoring, logging, and alerting.","From memory, list the four things that can drift in a deployed model. Check your list against today's notes afterward."],"time":"0.5h"},{"label":"New material","steps":["Read the bli55 notes on monitoring (mlops/mlops-monitoring.md) — focus on the four Model Monitor types and what each compares.","Read the ccsusan mlops-monitoring.md quick reference; copy the baseline → schedule → violation flow into your own words.","In the SageMaker docs, find the Model Monitor page and note what a monitoring schedule emits: violation reports in S3 plus CloudWatch metrics.","Write the one-sentence difference between data drift and concept drift — the exam loves this pair."],"time":"2h"},{"code":"import boto3\n\ncw = boto3.client(\"cloudwatch\", region_name=\"us-east-1\")\ncw.put_metric_alarm(\n    AlarmName=\"sagemaker-endpoint-5xx\",\n    MetricName=\"Invocation5XXErrors\",\n    Namespace=\"AWS/SageMaker\",\n    Statistic=\"Sum\",\n    Period=300,\n    EvaluationPeriods=1,\n    Threshold=1,\n    ComparisonOperator=\"GreaterThanOrEqualToThreshold\",\n    Dimensions=[\n        {\"Name\": \"EndpointName\", \"Value\": \"my-endpoint\"},\n        {\"Name\": \"VariantName\", \"Value\": \"AllTraffic\"},\n    ],\n    AlarmActions=[\"arn:aws:sns:us-east-1:ACCOUNT_ID:ml-alerts\"],\n)\nprint(\"Alarm created — delete it after the lab if you do not need it.\")","codeLang":"python","label":"Hands-on lab","steps":["In CloudWatch Metrics, browse the AWS/SageMaker namespace and identify Invocations, ModelLatency, and error metrics for an endpoint.","Run the snippet below to create a CloudWatch alarm on Invocation5XXErrors with an SNS notification (replace the SNS ARN with your own topic).","On paper, draw the baseline → schedule → violation → alarm flow; label where S3 and CloudWatch sit in it.","Clean up: delete the alarm after the lab unless you want to keep it. Create no endpoints in this lab — nothing else to delete."],"time":"1.5h"},{"label":"Practice + teach-it-back","steps":["Do 20 practice questions on monitoring from the ihatesea69 repo.","Teach-it-back, out loud with notes closed: why does Model Quality monitoring need ground-truth labels, and where do those labels come from?"],"time":"1h"}],"day":21,"domain":"Domain 4: Operating, Monitoring, Securing (24%)","isCheckpoint":false,"isReview":false,"keyTerms":[{"def":"SageMaker's continuous monitoring for endpoints; compares live traffic against a baseline.","term":"Model Monitor"},{"def":"Statistics and constraints computed from training data — the 'normal' production is compared against.","term":"Baseline"},{"def":"Production input distribution shifts away from training data.","term":"Data drift"},{"def":"The input-to-label relationship itself changes; harder to catch, needs fresh labels.","term":"Concept drift"},{"def":"Human-verified correct labels, merged with predictions to compute model-quality metrics.","term":"Ground truth"},{"def":"Feature-attribution method; the explainability monitor watches for attribution drift.","term":"SHAP"},{"def":"Watches a metric and fires an action (e.g., SNS notification) when a threshold is breached.","term":"CloudWatch alarm"}],"notes":[{"heading":"The four Model Monitor types","points":["`Data Quality`: watches for schema changes and statistical drift in incoming features versus the baseline (built on Deequ-style constraints).","`Model Quality`: watches accuracy/precision/recall drift — the only type that needs `ground truth` labels merged with predictions.","`Model Bias`: watches Clarify bias metrics (e.g., disparate impact) drifting outside the baseline's acceptable range.","`Model Explainability`: watches `SHAP` feature-attribution drift — which features the model relies on has changed.","Exam shortcut: three types compare against the baseline alone; only Model Quality needs fresh human-verified labels."]},{"heading":"Baselines and schedules","points":["A baseline job (`suggest_baseline`) runs over training/validation data and writes statistics plus constraints to S3.","A monitoring schedule runs on a cron expression, analyzes recent production data, and writes violation reports to S3.","Each run also emits CloudWatch metrics, so violations can trigger alarms without anyone reading the S3 reports.","Baselines go stale: re-run the baseline job after retraining, or you will alarm on your own improved model."]},{"heading":"Data drift vs concept drift","points":["`Data drift`: the input distribution shifted — P(X) changed. Caught by the Data Quality monitor.","`Concept drift`: the inputs look the same but the right answers changed — P(y|X) changed. Shows up in Model Quality, needs fresh labels.","Example: loan applicants' income distribution shifts (data drift) vs. what counts as creditworthy changes after a policy update (concept drift).","The exam often pairs them as a two-option trap — anchor on whether the inputs changed or the relationship changed."]},{"heading":"CloudWatch for ML","points":["Endpoint metrics live in the `AWS/SageMaker` namespace: `Invocations`, `Invocation4XXErrors`, `Invocation5XXErrors`, `ModelLatency`, `OverheadLatency`.","Alarms watch a metric and fire actions (SNS notification, EventBridge event) when a threshold breaches.","`CloudWatch Logs` captures training-job logs and endpoint container logs — first stop when a job fails.","Dashboards combine metrics, alarms, and log queries into one operational view of an endpoint."]},{"heading":"The violation response loop","points":["Violation detected → CloudWatch metric → alarm → SNS/EventBridge notification.","Response options: page a human, trigger a retraining pipeline (Day 22), or roll the endpoint back to a previous model version.","The exam's favorite phrasing: `automatically retrain when drift is detected` — that is Model Monitor plus EventBridge plus SageMaker Pipelines.","Monitoring without a response plan is just expensive logging — the loop is the point."]}],"objectives":["Explain the four SageMaker Model Monitor types and what drift each one catches","Describe how baselines and monitoring schedules work, and where violations surface","Use CloudWatch metrics, alarms, and logs to observe a SageMaker endpoint in production"],"quiz":[{"answer":1,"options":["Data Quality","Model Quality","Model Bias","Model Explainability"],"q":"Which SageMaker Model Monitor type requires ground-truth labels merged with predictions?","why":"Only model-quality metrics (accuracy, precision, recall) need true labels; the other three compare against the baseline alone."},{"answer":1,"options":["Data drift","Concept drift","Schema drift","Bias drift"],"q":"A monitoring schedule reports that feature distributions match training, but accuracy is steadily falling. What drift is this?","why":"Inputs unchanged but the input-to-label relationship moved — classic concept drift."},{"answer":1,"options":["Live production traffic sampled for one hour","A baseline job run over training/validation data, stored in S3","Manually written thresholds in CloudWatch","The endpoint's CloudWatch Logs"],"q":"Where does a Model Monitor baseline come from?","why":"The baseline job computes statistics and constraints from training data; everything later is compared to it."},{"answer":1,"options":["It emails the account root automatically","It emits CloudWatch metrics you alarm on, e.g., routed to SNS","It stops the endpoint","It opens an AWS Support case"],"q":"Model Monitor finds a violation. How does the on-call team get notified?","why":"Model Monitor publishes metrics and S3 reports; notification is your CloudWatch alarm to SNS or EventBridge wiring."},{"answer":2,"options":["Invocations","ModelLatency","Invocation5XXErrors","CPUUtilization"],"q":"Which CloudWatch metric best catches an endpoint returning server-side errors to callers?","why":"5XX means server-side invocation errors — alarm on it for paging."}],"resources":[{"label":"MLA-C02 exam guide (PDF)","note":"Domain 4 task statements — know exactly which monitoring verbs the exam tests.","url":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/pdfs/aws-certification/latest/machine-learning-engineer-associate-02/machine-learning-engineer-associate-02.pdf"},{"label":"bli55 notes","note":"mlops/mlops-monitoring.md — the four Model Monitor types with exam framing.","url":"https://github.com/bli55/notes-aws-machine-learning"},{"label":"ccsusan notes","note":"mlops-monitoring.md quick reference and cheat-sheet tables.","url":"https://github.com/ccsusan/aws-mla-certification-notes"},{"label":"SageMaker docs","note":"Model Monitor page: baselines, schedules, and violation outputs.","url":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/sagemaker/"},{"label":"AWS Skill Builder","note":"Official monitoring walkthroughs if the notes feel thin.","url":"https://skillbuilder.aws"}],"title":"Model Monitor & CloudWatch","week":5},{"blocks":[{"label":"Warm-up","steps":["From memory, rewrite the Day 21 violation loop. Circle the step where retraining the model would plug in.","Skim the exam guide Domain 4 tasks for the words retrain, pipeline, and human."],"time":"0.5h"},{"label":"New material","steps":["Read bli55 mlops-cicd.md: SageMaker Pipelines steps, Model Registry, approval gates.","Read ccsusan mlops-cicd.md plus the A2I portion of aws-ai-services.md.","Learn the four retraining triggers cold: scheduled, drift/violation-driven, new-data-driven, performance-degradation.","Diagram on paper: EventBridge (monitor violation) to retraining pipeline to registry to manual approval to endpoint update."],"time":"2h"},{"code":"import boto3\n\nsm = boto3.client(\"sagemaker\", region_name=\"us-east-1\")\n\n# The human checkpoint in an automated pipeline:\n# a model version sits at PendingManualApproval until someone approves it.\nsm.update_model_package(\n    ModelPackageArn=\"arn:aws:sagemaker:us-east-1:ACCOUNT_ID:model-package/my-group/1\",\n    ModelApprovalStatus=\"Approved\",\n)\nprint(\"Model version approved — CI/CD may now deploy it.\")","codeLang":"python","label":"Hands-on lab","steps":["In the SageMaker console, open SageMaker Pipelines and inspect a sample pipeline definition's steps: processing to training to evaluation to register.","Run the snippet below to practice the registry approval gate — the human checkpoint inside an automated pipeline (use a real model-package ARN from your account).","On paper, sketch an A2I flow: prediction to confidence below threshold to human review task to verified label in S3 to ground truth for monitoring and retraining.","Cost check: this lab creates no endpoints or training jobs — nothing to delete."],"time":"1.5h"},{"label":"Practice + teach-it-back","steps":["Do 20 practice questions on MLOps and CI/CD from the ihatesea69 repo.","Teach-it-back, out loud with notes closed: why does a registry approval gate exist — what specific failure does it prevent?"],"time":"1h"}],"day":22,"domain":"Domain 4: Operating, Monitoring, Securing (24%)","isCheckpoint":false,"isReview":false,"keyTerms":[{"def":"An event or schedule that starts a new training run.","term":"Retraining trigger"},{"def":"Managed ML workflow orchestration with steps for processing, training, evaluation, and registration.","term":"SageMaker Pipelines"},{"def":"Versioned catalog of candidate models with metrics, lineage, and approval status.","term":"Model Registry"},{"def":"Manual Approved/Rejected checkpoint a model must pass before it can deploy.","term":"Approval gate"},{"def":"Event bus that routes Model Monitor violations into pipeline executions.","term":"EventBridge"},{"def":"Human review workflows for low-confidence ML predictions in production.","term":"A2I (Augmented AI)"},{"def":"Human-verified labels flowing back into evaluation, monitoring, and retraining.","term":"Ground-truth loop"}],"notes":[{"heading":"Retraining triggers","points":["`Scheduled`: retrain on a cadence (weekly, monthly) as fresh data accumulates — simplest, most common.","`Drift/violation-driven`: a Model Monitor violation fires an EventBridge event that starts the pipeline.","`New-data-driven`: retrain when enough new labeled data lands in S3 to matter.","`Performance-degradation`: a business or model-quality metric slips below its target.","Exam phrasing to recognize: `automatically retrain when data drift is detected`."]},{"heading":"SageMaker Pipelines","points":["A pipeline is a DAG of steps: processing (feature prep), training, evaluation, condition check, register model, optionally deploy.","Pipelines are defined in the SDK, versioned, and re-runnable — they separate experiment code from production orchestration.","The evaluation step can gate registration: only register the model if metrics beat the current production version.","Retraining is just re-executing the pipeline with fresh data — same code, new inputs."]},{"heading":"Model Registry and approval gates","points":["The registry versions every candidate model with its metrics, artifacts, and lineage.","Approval status moves `PendingManualApproval` to `Approved` or `Rejected` — a human decision.","CI/CD deploys only `Approved` versions, which stops a bad automatic retrain from ever reaching production.","This is the exam's answer whenever a question wants automation with a human safety check on deployment."]},{"heading":"CodePipeline for ML","points":["CodePipeline orchestrates the outer loop: source change (code or config) to build/test to pipeline execution to deploy.","EventBridge wires monitor violations into pipeline starts, closing the Day 21 loop.","Keep the layers straight: CodePipeline orchestrates releases; SageMaker Pipelines orchestrates ML workflows."]},{"heading":"A2I: human-in-the-loop","points":["`A2I` (Augmented AI) routes low-confidence predictions to human reviewers — a private workforce, a vendor team, or Mechanical Turk.","Humans verify or correct the prediction; the verified labels land in S3.","Those labels become ground truth for evaluation, for Day 21-style model-quality monitoring, and for retraining.","Reach for A2I when errors are costly: content moderation, document extraction review, anything where a wrong prediction has consequences.","Do not confuse with Ground Truth: Ground Truth labels raw data for training; A2I reviews live predictions in production."]}],"objectives":["List the four standard retraining triggers and match each to a detection mechanism","Sketch a SageMaker CI/CD loop: pipeline to registry to approval to deploy","Explain when and how A2I inserts humans into a production ML workflow"],"quiz":[{"answer":1,"options":["CloudTrail","EventBridge","KMS","Athena"],"q":"A Model Monitor data-quality violation should automatically start retraining. Which service routes the violation event to the pipeline?","why":"EventBridge consumes the monitor's events and triggers the SageMaker Pipeline execution."},{"answer":1,"options":["Inside the training script","The Model Registry approval status","The S3 bucket policy","CloudWatch Logs"],"q":"Where does the human checkpoint live in SageMaker MLOps?","why":"Registry versions sit at PendingManualApproval until a human marks them Approved — only then can CI/CD deploy."},{"answer":3,"options":["Scheduled cadence","Drift violation","New labeled data","Changing the IAM execution role"],"q":"Which is NOT a standard retraining trigger?","why":"IAM changes affect permissions, not model freshness; the other three are the classic triggers."},{"answer":1,"options":["Training data needs labeling at scale from scratch","Low-confidence predictions need human verification before acting","You need faster GPU training","Endpoints need VPC isolation"],"q":"A2I is the right choice when…","why":"A2I is human-in-the-loop review of live predictions, not a labeling service (that is Ground Truth) or infrastructure."},{"answer":1,"options":["Billing records only","Ground truth for evaluation, monitoring, and retraining","Endpoint auto-scaling decisions","KMS key rotation"],"q":"Verified A2I labels are stored in S3 and then used for…","why":"Human-verified labels close the loop — they become the ground truth that model-quality monitoring needs."}],"resources":[{"label":"MLA-C02 exam guide (PDF)","note":"Domain 4 tasks on retraining, pipelines, and human review.","url":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/pdfs/aws-certification/latest/machine-learning-engineer-associate-02/machine-learning-engineer-associate-02.pdf"},{"label":"bli55 notes","note":"mlops/mlops-cicd.md — pipelines, registry, and approval gates.","url":"https://github.com/bli55/notes-aws-machine-learning"},{"label":"ccsusan notes","note":"mlops-cicd.md plus the A2I section of aws-ai-services.md.","url":"https://github.com/ccsusan/aws-mla-certification-notes"},{"label":"AWS Skill Builder","note":"Official MLOps and human-in-the-loop walkthroughs.","url":"https://skillbuilder.aws"}],"title":"Retraining Triggers, CI/CD & Human-in-the-Loop","week":5},{"blocks":[{"label":"Warm-up","steps":["List every AWS service a SageMaker training job touches (S3, ECR, CloudWatch Logs, KMS, VPC). For each, ask: what permission does the job actually need?","Re-read the exam guide Domain 4 security tasks and underline each distinct security control."],"time":"0.5h"},{"label":"New material","steps":["Read ccsusan security.md and security-encryption.md: IAM roles vs policies, KMS concepts, SSE-S3 vs SSE-KMS.","Read the bli55 security notes: execution roles, trust policies, key policies.","Learn the two policy types cold: identity-based (attached to a role or user) vs resource-based (attached to a bucket or key).","Memorize the two exam-favorite S3 conditions: s3:x-amz-server-side-encryption and aws:SecureTransport."],"time":"2h"},{"code":"import boto3\nimport json\n\ns3 = boto3.client(\"s3\", region_name=\"us-east-1\")\nbucket = \"my-ml-test-bucket\"  # replace with your test bucket\n\npolicy = {\n    \"Version\": \"2012-10-17\",\n    \"Statement\": [{\n        \"Sid\": \"DenyUnencryptedUploads\",\n        \"Effect\": \"Deny\",\n        \"Principal\": \"*\",\n        \"Action\": \"s3:PutObject\",\n        \"Resource\": \"arn:aws:s3:::\" + bucket + \"/*\",\n        \"Condition\": {\n            \"StringNotEquals\": {\"s3:x-amz-server-side-encryption\": \"aws:kms\"}\n        },\n    }],\n}\ns3.put_bucket_policy(Bucket=bucket, Policy=json.dumps(policy))\nprint(\"Policy attached — test an unencrypted upload, then remove the policy.\")","codeLang":"python","label":"Hands-on lab","steps":["In the console, create a symmetric KMS key for S3/SageMaker use and read its key policy — note who can use the key vs administer it.","Run the snippet below to attach a deny-unencrypted-uploads bucket policy to a test bucket, then verify an unencrypted put is denied.","Inspect a SageMaker execution role: read its trust policy (the sagemaker.amazonaws.com principal) and its attached permissions.","Clean up: remove the test bucket policy or delete the test bucket. KMS keys cost about $1/month — schedule deletion if you created one just for practice."],"time":"1.5h"},{"label":"Practice + teach-it-back","steps":["Do 20 security questions from the ihatesea69 repo.","Teach-it-back, out loud with notes closed: why does a training job use an execution role instead of your user credentials?"],"time":"1h"}],"day":23,"domain":"Domain 4: Operating, Monitoring, Securing (24%)","isCheckpoint":false,"isReview":false,"keyTerms":[{"def":"IAM role that SageMaker assumes to act on your behalf in jobs and endpoints.","term":"Execution role"},{"def":"The policy stating who is allowed to assume a role, e.g., the SageMaker service principal.","term":"Trust policy"},{"def":"Granting only the actions on only the resources a workload needs — nothing more.","term":"Least privilege"},{"def":"Managed encryption keys; you control who may use and administer each key via key policies.","term":"KMS"},{"def":"S3 server-side encryption with a customer-managed KMS key.","term":"SSE-KMS"},{"def":"Resource-based policy attached to an S3 bucket.","term":"Bucket policy"},{"def":"Condition key that is true only for HTTPS/TLS requests.","term":"aws:SecureTransport"}],"notes":[{"heading":"SageMaker execution roles","points":["An execution role is an IAM role that SageMaker assumes on your behalf for training jobs, endpoints, and notebook instances.","The `trust policy` lets the `sagemaker.amazonaws.com` service principal assume the role.","The `permissions policy` grants exactly the S3, ECR, CloudWatch Logs, KMS, and VPC actions the workload needs.","Never embed long-lived user access keys in training code — the role provides temporary, scoped credentials instead."]},{"heading":"Least privilege in practice","points":["Scope S3 permissions to the specific data and model buckets, not `s3:*`.","Use separate roles for training, inference, and notebooks — a compromised notebook should not be able to retrain models.","Permission boundaries let delegated admins create roles without exceeding a maximum privilege ceiling."]},{"heading":"KMS: encryption at rest","points":["`SSE-S3`: S3 encrypts with AWS-managed keys — simple, but you do not control the key.","`SSE-KMS`: S3 encrypts with a KMS key you manage — required when the organization must control or audit key usage.","SageMaker training volumes and model artifacts can be encrypted with your KMS key via `VolumeKmsKeyId` and related settings.","`Key policies` are resource-based: they decide who may use versus administer each key."]},{"heading":"Encryption in transit","points":["Endpoint invocations, console access, and API calls use TLS by default.","A bucket policy with `aws:SecureTransport` set to false and effect `Deny` blocks any non-HTTPS S3 access.","Exam pattern: `all traffic must be encrypted in transit` is almost always this condition."]},{"heading":"S3 bucket policies that show up on exams","points":["Bucket policies are `resource-based` JSON attached to the bucket itself.","Pattern 1: deny `s3:PutObject` when `s3:x-amz-server-side-encryption` is not `aws:kms` — enforces KMS encryption on upload.","Pattern 2: deny all actions when `aws:SecureTransport` is false — enforces TLS.","Pattern 3: restrict access to a VPC endpoint via `aws:sourceVpce` — keeps bucket traffic private (see Day 24)."]}],"objectives":["Explain what a SageMaker execution role is and apply least privilege to it","Describe KMS encryption at rest and in transit for SageMaker workloads","State the intent of an S3 bucket policy that enforces encryption and TLS"],"quiz":[{"answer":1,"options":["Embed your IAM user access keys in the training script","Assume an execution role with scoped S3 permissions","Make the bucket public during training","Use the account root credentials"],"q":"A SageMaker training job needs to read training data from S3. How should it authenticate?","why":"Execution roles give the job temporary, scoped credentials — never long-lived keys in code."},{"answer":1,"options":["aws:SecureTransport","s3:x-amz-server-side-encryption","kms:ViaService","s3:ExistingObjectTag"],"q":"Which bucket-policy condition enforces that uploads use KMS encryption?","why":"That condition key carries the request's SSE setting; deny the put when it is not aws:kms."},{"answer":1,"options":["s3:x-amz-server-side-encryption","aws:SecureTransport evaluated as true","aws:sourceVpce","kms:GrantIsForAWSResource"],"q":"A company requires all S3 access over HTTPS. Which condition enforces it?","why":"Denying when aws:SecureTransport is false is the textbook TLS-enforcement pattern."},{"answer":1,"options":["EnableNetworkIsolation","VolumeKmsKeyId","Subnets","RoleArn"],"q":"Training on sensitive data requires the training instances' storage volumes to be encrypted with a company-managed key. Which setting selects the key?","why":"VolumeKmsKeyId selects the KMS key used for the training instance's EBS volume."},{"answer":1,"options":["Bucket policies are identity-based; role policies are resource-based","Role policies are identity-based; bucket policies and KMS key policies are resource-based","They are the same thing with different names","Only IAM users can have policies attached"],"q":"Identity-based vs resource-based policy — which statement is correct?","why":"Identity-based policies attach to who (user/role); resource-based policies attach to what (bucket, KMS key)."}],"resources":[{"label":"MLA-C02 exam guide (PDF)","note":"Domain 4 security tasks — the exact controls the exam expects you to pick.","url":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/pdfs/aws-certification/latest/machine-learning-engineer-associate-02/machine-learning-engineer-associate-02.pdf"},{"label":"ccsusan notes","note":"security.md and security-encryption.md — roles, KMS, and SSE-S3 vs SSE-KMS tables.","url":"https://github.com/ccsusan/aws-mla-certification-notes"},{"label":"bli55 notes","note":"Security notes on execution roles, trust policies, and key policies.","url":"https://github.com/bli55/notes-aws-machine-learning"},{"label":"SageMaker docs","note":"Execution-role requirements and KMS encryption options for training and endpoints.","url":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/sagemaker/"}],"title":"IAM Roles, KMS & S3 Bucket Policies","week":5},{"blocks":[{"label":"Warm-up","steps":["Redraw yesterday's execution-role picture and add a box for network: what can the training container reach by default?","Skim the exam guide Domain 4 tasks mentioning VPC, PrivateLink, and CloudTrail."],"time":"0.5h"},{"label":"New material","steps":["Read ccsusan security-network.md: SageMaker VPC config, security groups, and endpoints.","Read the bli55 security/networking notes on PrivateLink and network isolation.","Learn the decision trio cold: network isolation (no network at all) vs VPC endpoints (private AWS access) vs NAT gateway (controlled internet egress).","Learn what a CloudTrail event contains: who, what action, which resource, when, and from where."],"time":"2h"},{"code":"import boto3\n\nct = boto3.client(\"cloudtrail\", region_name=\"us-east-1\")\nfor t in ct.describe_trails()[\"trailList\"]:\n    print(t[\"Name\"],\n          \"multi-region:\", t.get(\"IsMultiRegionTrail\"),\n          \"validation:\", t.get(\"LogFileValidationEnabled\"))","codeLang":"python","label":"Hands-on lab","steps":["In the SageMaker console, inspect a notebook instance's network settings and note the subnets and security-group fields.","Run the snippet below to audit your CloudTrail trails: are they multi-region, and is log-file validation enabled?","On paper, design this: a training job with network isolation enabled, an S3 gateway VPC endpoint, no NAT — label every traffic path as allowed or blocked.","Cost check: this lab creates no billable resources — nothing to delete."],"time":"1.5h"},{"label":"Practice + teach-it-back","steps":["Do 20 networking and security questions from the ihatesea69 repo.","Teach-it-back, out loud with notes closed: a compliance rule says training containers must have zero network access — which setting, and what breaks?"],"time":"1h"}],"day":24,"domain":"Domain 4: Operating, Monitoring, Securing (24%)","isCheckpoint":false,"isReview":false,"keyTerms":[{"def":"Subnets and security groups attached to SageMaker resources, placing them on your private network.","term":"VPC config"},{"def":"Training containers run with all network access disabled.","term":"Network isolation"},{"def":"Private connection from your VPC to an AWS service with no internet traversal.","term":"VPC endpoint (PrivateLink)"},{"def":"Free, S3- and DynamoDB-specific VPC endpoint type.","term":"Gateway endpoint"},{"def":"Managed outbound-only internet access for private subnets; billed hourly plus per GB.","term":"NAT gateway"},{"def":"Audit log of AWS API activity: who did what, to which resource, and when.","term":"CloudTrail"},{"def":"CloudTrail integrity check proving log files were not tampered with.","term":"Log file validation"}],"notes":[{"heading":"SageMaker in a VPC","points":["Attach training jobs, endpoints, and notebook instances to private subnets plus security groups via the VPC config.","They get elastic network interfaces in your VPC and no public IPs.","By default there is no internet route — outbound internet requires a NAT gateway in the design.","Security groups control which ports and peers each resource can talk to."]},{"heading":"Network isolation","points":["`EnableNetworkIsolation=True` gives training containers zero network access — no internet, no AWS API calls.","The SageMaker platform still stages input data onto the instance, so training on S3 data keeps working.","Use it for the strictest data-governance requirements, where even the training code must not exfiltrate.","Exam signal: `containers must not access the network` or `no internet access under any circumstance`."]},{"heading":"PrivateLink and VPC endpoints","points":["`Interface endpoints` (PrivateLink) keep traffic to AWS services — SageMaker API, SageMaker Runtime — off the public internet.","The `gateway endpoint` for S3 is free and is the exam's standard answer for private S3 access from a VPC.","Exam signal: `access AWS services without traversing the internet` points at VPC endpoints, not NAT.","Endpoints have their own policies, so you can further restrict which actions flow through them."]},{"heading":"NAT gateway","points":["A NAT gateway lets private-subnet resources initiate outbound internet (package installs, external APIs) with no inbound exposure.","It bills hourly plus per gigabyte processed — the exam's cost questions bring it up for a reason.","If the workload only needs AWS services, prefer VPC endpoints over NAT: private and cheaper.","Exam signal: `private subnet needs outbound internet only`."]},{"heading":"CloudTrail auditing","points":["CloudTrail records AWS API calls: identity, action, resource, timestamp, and source IP.","Trails deliver log files to S3; `log file validation` proves the logs were not tampered with.","A multi-region trail captures activity in every region from one place.","Answers the exam's favorite audit question: `who changed the endpoint configuration, and when?`"]}],"objectives":["Place SageMaker training and inference inside a VPC and explain what changes","Choose between network isolation, VPC endpoints, and NAT for a traffic requirement","Describe what CloudTrail records and how it supports auditing"],"quiz":[{"answer":1,"options":["Place them in a public subnet","Enable network isolation","Add a NAT gateway","Attach a KMS key"],"q":"Training containers must have no network access of any kind. Which setting enforces this?","why":"Network isolation cuts all container networking; the platform still stages input data."},{"answer":1,"options":["A NAT gateway","An interface VPC endpoint (PrivateLink) for SageMaker Runtime","A public IP on the endpoint","An internet gateway"],"q":"An endpoint in a private VPC must call the SageMaker Runtime API without internet access. What do you add?","why":"Interface endpoints keep AWS API traffic private inside the VPC."},{"answer":2,"options":["A NAT gateway","An interface VPC endpoint","An S3 gateway VPC endpoint","A VPN connection"],"q":"What is the cheapest way for private-subnet training to read training data from S3?","why":"Gateway endpoints for S3 are free and keep traffic on the AWS network."},{"answer":1,"options":["CloudWatch Metrics","CloudTrail","GuardDuty","Athena"],"q":"Who deleted the production endpoint yesterday at 3 PM? Which service answers that question?","why":"CloudTrail records the API call with identity, action, resource, and timestamp."},{"answer":1,"options":["An internet gateway on the subnet","A NAT gateway","Network isolation","A public subnet"],"q":"A private subnet needs package installs during training — outbound internet only, no inbound exposure. What fits?","why":"NAT gives outbound-only internet to private subnets; an internet gateway would require public IPs."}],"resources":[{"label":"MLA-C02 exam guide (PDF)","note":"Domain 4 tasks on VPC design, PrivateLink, and auditing.","url":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/pdfs/aws-certification/latest/machine-learning-engineer-associate-02/machine-learning-engineer-associate-02.pdf"},{"label":"ccsusan notes","note":"security-network.md — SageMaker VPC config, security groups, endpoints.","url":"https://github.com/ccsusan/aws-mla-certification-notes"},{"label":"bli55 notes","note":"Security/networking notes on PrivateLink and network isolation.","url":"https://github.com/bli55/notes-aws-machine-learning"},{"label":"AWS Skill Builder","note":"Official networking and auditing walkthroughs for ML workloads.","url":"https://skillbuilder.aws"}],"title":"VPC Networking, PrivateLink & CloudTrail","week":5},{"blocks":[{"label":"Morning sweep","steps":["Re-read your notes from days 21–24, one day at a time; star anything you cannot explain in one sentence.","Rebuild the four Model Monitor types from memory, then check against Day 21.","Rebuild the retraining-trigger list and the A2I flow from memory, then check against Day 22."],"time":"1h"},{"label":"Security drill","steps":["From memory: execution role vs bucket policy vs key policy — which attaches to what?","Write the two S3 bucket-policy patterns (SSE-KMS enforcement, TLS enforcement) from memory.","From memory: network isolation vs VPC endpoint vs NAT gateway — when is each the answer?"],"time":"1.5h"},{"label":"Re-lab the weak spots","steps":["Re-run any lab step from days 21–24 that felt shaky; labs are where exam answers stick.","If CloudWatch alarms or bucket policies were fuzzy, redo those snippets in a scratch setup and delete afterward.","Cost check: delete any alarm, policy, or bucket you recreated for practice."],"time":"1.5h"},{"label":"Cumulative quiz + teach-it-back","steps":["Take the 10-question quiz below closed-book; target 8 or more correct.","For every miss, write the one-line rule you would tell a teammate, then re-quiz yourself on just the misses.","Teach-it-back: narrate the full Week 5 story out loud — monitor detects drift, violation fires, pipeline retrains, registry approves, endpoint updates, all wrapped in roles, KMS, VPC controls, and audited by CloudTrail."],"time":"1h"}],"day":25,"domain":"Domain 4: Operating, Monitoring, Securing (24%)","isCheckpoint":false,"isReview":true,"keyTerms":[{"def":"Week 21 recap: continuous endpoint monitoring against a baseline; four types.","term":"Model Monitor"},{"def":"Week 21 recap: input distribution shifted away from training data.","term":"Data drift"},{"def":"Week 21 recap: the input-to-label relationship changed; needs fresh labels to detect.","term":"Concept drift"},{"def":"Week 22 recap: versioned candidate models with metrics and approval status.","term":"Model Registry"},{"def":"Week 22 recap: human Approved/Rejected checkpoint before deployment.","term":"Approval gate"},{"def":"Week 22 recap: human review of low-confidence live predictions.","term":"A2I"},{"def":"Week 23 recap: IAM role SageMaker assumes for jobs and endpoints.","term":"Execution role"},{"def":"Week 23 recap: S3 encryption with a customer-managed KMS key.","term":"SSE-KMS"},{"def":"Week 23 recap: resource-based policy on an S3 bucket; enforces encryption and TLS patterns.","term":"Bucket policy"},{"def":"Week 24 recap: training containers with all network access disabled.","term":"Network isolation"},{"def":"Week 24 recap: private AWS-service access without internet; gateway (S3, free) vs interface (PrivateLink).","term":"VPC endpoint"},{"def":"Week 24 recap: audit log of AWS API activity.","term":"CloudTrail"}],"notes":[{"heading":"How to use this review day","points":["No new material today — retrieval only. Reading feels productive; recall is productive.","Every block starts from memory and checks against notes, never the reverse.","Anything you miss twice goes on tomorrow's weak-areas list verbatim."]},{"heading":"The Week 5 story in one flow","points":["Model Monitor watches the endpoint against its baseline: data quality, model quality, bias, explainability.","A violation emits CloudWatch metrics; alarms and EventBridge turn detection into action.","EventBridge starts a SageMaker Pipeline retrain; the new model lands in the registry at PendingManualApproval.","A human approves; CI/CD deploys. A2I reviewers feed verified labels back as ground truth.","All of it runs under execution roles, KMS encryption, VPC controls, and CloudTrail auditing."]},{"heading":"Common mix-ups to settle today","points":["Model Monitor types: only Model Quality needs ground-truth labels.","Drift types: inputs changed (data drift) vs relationship changed (concept drift).","A2I reviews live predictions; Ground Truth labels raw data for training.","SSE-S3 uses AWS-managed keys; SSE-KMS uses keys you manage.","Identity-based policies attach to who; resource-based attach to what.","Gateway endpoints are free and S3-specific; interface endpoints use PrivateLink per service."]}],"objectives":["Recall the monitoring to retraining to security chain end to end without notes","Close every gap flagged during days 21–24","Score 8+/10 on the cumulative quiz before moving to Week 6"],"quiz":[{"answer":1,"options":["Data Quality","Model Quality","Model Bias","Model Explainability"],"q":"Which Model Monitor type requires ground-truth labels merged with predictions?","why":"Only model-quality metrics need true labels; the rest compare against the baseline."},{"answer":1,"options":["Data drift","Concept drift","Schema drift","Latency drift"],"q":"Inputs look identical to training, but accuracy is falling after a policy change. What drift is this?","why":"Same inputs, different right answers — the relationship changed, so concept drift."},{"answer":1,"options":["CloudTrail","EventBridge","KMS","SNS topic alone"],"q":"A drift violation should automatically trigger a retraining pipeline. Which service carries the event?","why":"EventBridge routes the monitor's events into a SageMaker Pipeline execution."},{"answer":1,"options":["Ground Truth","A2I","Rekognition","Comprehend"],"q":"Low-confidence fraud predictions must be verified by people before money moves. Which service fits?","why":"A2I is human review of live predictions; Ground Truth labels raw data for training."},{"answer":1,"options":["The IAM user who created the notebook","sagemaker.amazonaws.com","The S3 bucket","The KMS key"],"q":"The trust policy of a SageMaker execution role must allow which principal to assume it?","why":"The trust policy names the SageMaker service principal as the trusted entity."},{"answer":1,"options":["aws:SecureTransport","s3:x-amz-server-side-encryption","aws:sourceVpce","s3:signatureversion"],"q":"Which bucket-policy condition enforces KMS encryption on uploads?","why":"Deny the put when this condition is not aws:kms."},{"answer":1,"options":["Public subnet","Network isolation","NAT gateway","Interface endpoint"],"q":"Compliance requires training containers with zero network access. Which setting?","why":"EnableNetworkIsolation removes all container networking."},{"answer":2,"options":["NAT gateway","Interface endpoint","S3 gateway VPC endpoint","Internet gateway"],"q":"Private-subnet resources need S3 access with no internet and no extra cost. What do you use?","why":"The S3 gateway endpoint is free and keeps traffic on the AWS network."},{"answer":1,"options":["CloudWatch Metrics","CloudTrail","Config is not on the exam","KMS"],"q":"Which service tells you who modified an endpoint configuration last Tuesday?","why":"CloudTrail logs the API call with identity, action, and timestamp."},{"answer":1,"options":["The CloudWatch alarm","The Model Registry approval gate","The bucket policy","The VPC endpoint policy"],"q":"An automated retrain produced a worse model. What stops it from reaching production?","why":"Versions wait at PendingManualApproval until a human approves them."}],"resources":[{"label":"MLA-C02 exam guide (PDF)","note":"Domain 4 tasks — confirm you can answer each one from memory.","url":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/pdfs/aws-certification/latest/machine-learning-engineer-associate-02/machine-learning-engineer-associate-02.pdf"},{"label":"ccsusan cheat sheet","note":"Quick tables covering the week's terms; read it as a self-test.","url":"https://github.com/ccsusan/aws-mla-certification-notes"},{"label":"bli55 notes","note":"Deeper pages for any topic the cheat sheet cannot settle.","url":"https://github.com/bli55/notes-aws-machine-learning"}],"title":"Week 5 Cumulative Review","week":5},{"blocks":[{"label":"Cheat-sheet pass","steps":["Read the ccsusan cheat sheet end to end; mark each row green (could teach it), yellow (fuzzy), or red (blank).","Do the same against the exam guide's task statements — every task should map to something you know.","Tally reds and yellows per domain; the domain with the most becomes tomorrow's priority."],"time":"1.5h"},{"label":"Weak-area triage","steps":["Write the weak-areas list: each item gets one line — topic, why it is weak, and the source you will repair it from.","Cap the list at about 10 items; if it is longer, keep only what the exam weights most (Domain 1 first, then the rest).","For each item, decide the repair type: re-read, re-lab, or drill questions."],"time":"1.5h"},{"label":"Logistics check","steps":["Confirm your exam booking: date, time, and test-center address or online-proctoring setup.","Check ID requirements (government-issued, name matching registration) and your test-day setup.","Note the format you will simulate tomorrow: 85 questions, 170 minutes, flag-and-return."],"time":"1h"},{"label":"Quiz + teach-it-back","steps":["Take the 5 commonly-confused questions below, closed book.","Pick your weakest domain and teach its core flow out loud for 10 minutes with notes closed."],"time":"1h"}],"day":26,"domain":"All domains (cumulative review)","isCheckpoint":false,"isReview":false,"keyTerms":[{"def":"Inputs changed vs the input-to-label relationship changed.","term":"Data drift vs concept drift"},{"def":"Human review of live predictions vs labeling raw data for training.","term":"A2I vs Ground Truth"},{"def":"AWS-managed encryption keys vs customer-managed KMS keys.","term":"SSE-S3 vs SSE-KMS"},{"def":"Free S3-specific endpoint vs per-service PrivateLink endpoint.","term":"Gateway vs interface endpoint"},{"def":"Tune and compare on validation; use test once for the final unbiased estimate.","term":"Validation set vs test set"}],"notes":[{"heading":"How to triage honestly","points":["`Green`: you could teach it to someone else right now.","`Yellow`: you recognize it but could not explain it — highest return on repair time.","`Red`: blank. Reds need re-reads; yellows usually just need question drills.","If everything feels yellow, you are being modest — force-rank and move on."]},{"heading":"The weak-areas list format","points":["One line per item: topic, symptom (what exactly fails), repair source, repair type.","Example: `SageMaker built-in algorithms — mix up XGBoost vs Linear Learner use cases — ccsusan aws-ml-algorithms.md — drill questions`.","Keep it visible tomorrow; it is the entire plan for Day 27."]},{"heading":"Weight-aware prioritization","points":["Domain 1 is 28% of the exam — its reds outrank everything else.","The three 24% domains tie; break ties by your personal miss rate on practice questions.","A yellow in Domain 1 beats a red in a topic the guide barely mentions."]},{"heading":"What done looks like today","points":["A written weak-areas list exists, capped and prioritized.","Every item has a repair type assigned for tomorrow.","Exam logistics are confirmed — no surprises left for test day."]}],"objectives":["Sweep all four domains' cheat sheets and rate every topic green, yellow, or red","Produce a written weak-areas list that drives days 27–28","Confirm exam logistics are on track (booking, ID, format)"],"quiz":[{"answer":1,"options":["SageMaker Ground Truth","Augmented AI (A2I)","SageMaker Clarify","Amazon Rekognition"],"q":"A team wants human review of low-confidence production predictions before acting. Which service?","why":"A2I reviews live predictions; Ground Truth labels raw data for training."},{"answer":1,"options":["SSE-S3","SSE-KMS","Client-side with no KMS","No encryption"],"q":"Compliance requires encryption with keys the company manages and audits. Which S3 option?","why":"SSE-KMS uses customer-managed KMS keys with key policies and CloudTrail logging."},{"answer":1,"options":["Interface endpoint","Gateway endpoint","NAT gateway","Transit gateway"],"q":"Private S3 access from a VPC at no additional cost. Which endpoint?","why":"The S3 gateway endpoint is free; interface endpoints bill hourly."},{"answer":1,"options":["Training set","Validation set","Test set","All data combined"],"q":"You are comparing three hyperparameter configurations. Which data split do you evaluate them on?","why":"Tune and compare on validation; the test set is used once, at the end."},{"answer":1,"options":["Real-time endpoint running 24/7","SageMaker Batch Transform","Serverless inference","Asynchronous inference"],"q":"A nightly job scores 10 million records from S3 and writes predictions back to S3. Most cost-effective inference?","why":"Batch Transform spins up for the job and shuts down — no idle endpoint cost for bulk offline scoring."}],"resources":[{"label":"ccsusan cheat sheet","note":"The primary triage document — read it as a self-test, row by row.","url":"https://github.com/ccsusan/aws-mla-certification-notes"},{"label":"MLA-C02 exam guide (PDF)","note":"Task statements to check coverage against; nothing unmapped should remain.","url":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/pdfs/aws-certification/latest/machine-learning-engineer-associate-02/machine-learning-engineer-associate-02.pdf"},{"label":"bli55 notes","note":"Repair source for any red that needs a deeper re-read.","url":"https://github.com/bli55/notes-aws-machine-learning"}],"title":"Cheat-Sheet Pass & Weak-Area Triage","week":6},{"blocks":[{"label":"Targeted repair — reds","steps":["Work the weak-areas list top-down; for each red, re-read the assigned source, then immediately do 5 questions on just that topic.","If a red needs a lab, run the smallest possible version (console or SDK) and clean up afterward — delete endpoints, remove policies.","Re-rate each item after repair; anything still red gets flagged for the Day 28 review session."],"time":"2h"},{"label":"Drills — yellows","steps":["Question drills only: 10 questions per yellow topic from the ihatesea69 repo or Skill Builder topic reviews.","For each miss, write the one-line rule; re-drill all misses at the end of the block.","Yellows that survive two drill rounds get promoted to the red repair loop above."],"time":"1.5h"},{"label":"Re-rate and plan","steps":["Rewrite the weak-areas list with new green/yellow/red ratings.","Note the 3 topics most likely to cost you points — these get final review on Day 29.","Confirm tomorrow's exam simulation setup: a quiet 170-minute block, no notes, phone away."],"time":"1h"},{"label":"Quiz","steps":["Take the 5 commonly-confused questions below, closed book.","Any miss joins the weak-areas list — no exceptions, no matter how small it feels."],"time":"0.5h"}],"day":27,"domain":"All domains (targeted repair)","isCheckpoint":false,"isReview":false,"keyTerms":[{"def":"Great train / poor validation vs poor on both.","term":"Overfitting vs underfitting signals"},{"def":"Precision punishes false alarms; recall punishes missed positives.","term":"Precision vs recall"},{"def":"Bulk offline scoring with no persistent endpoint vs always-on low-latency serving.","term":"Batch Transform vs real-time inference"},{"def":"Spare capacity at a discount (needs checkpointing) vs full-price guaranteed capacity.","term":"Spot vs on-demand training"},{"def":"Managed online/offline feature serving with point-in-time correctness vs raw object storage.","term":"Feature Store vs S3 for features"}],"notes":[{"heading":"The repair loop","points":["Diagnose precisely (which fact fails?) before re-reading — vague re-reading does not stick.","Smallest sufficient re-read, then immediate retrieval through questions.","Re-rate right away; memory of the repair fades within the hour if you do not test it.","One loop per item, then move on — you can loop again tomorrow if it is still red."]},{"heading":"Re-lab rules","points":["Labs prove concepts; they are not coverage exercises — smallest lab that demonstrates the idea wins.","Every AWS lab ends with cleanup: delete endpoints, stop notebook instances, remove test policies and alarms.","If a lab needs a billing alarm to feel safe, the $5 alarm from Week 0 is already watching."]},{"heading":"Knowing when to stop","points":["Diminishing returns set in after about two repair passes on one topic.","Stubborn items become exam-day guesses with a rule attached, not rabbit holes.","Protect the 170-minute simulation tomorrow — it outranks perfecting any single topic."]}],"objectives":["Repair every red item on the weak-areas list via re-read, re-lab, or drills","Convert yellows to greens with targeted question drills","Re-rate the list; carry only stubborn items into final exam review"],"quiz":[{"answer":1,"options":["Recall","Precision","F1-score always","AUC"],"q":"A fraud model flags legitimate transactions too often. Which metric directly punishes these false alarms?","why":"Precision measures correctness of positive predictions — false positives drag it down."},{"answer":1,"options":["Underfitting — train longer","Overfitting — regularize or add training data","Data leakage — shuffle the labels","High bias — use a simpler model"],"q":"Training accuracy is 99% but validation accuracy is 72%. What is happening and what is one fix?","why":"The train/validation gap is the signature of overfitting; regularization or more data closes it."},{"answer":1,"options":["Real-time endpoint","SageMaker Batch Transform","Serverless inference","Multi-model endpoint"],"q":"A CSV of customer records must be scored once per night, no latency requirement. Which inference option?","why":"Batch Transform is built for bulk offline scoring with no persistent endpoint cost."},{"answer":1,"options":["On-demand instances","Spot instances","Reserved instances","Larger instance type"],"q":"Training is fault-tolerant with checkpointing and cost matters most. Which capacity choice?","why":"Spot uses spare capacity at a discount; checkpointing survives interruptions."},{"answer":1,"options":["Raw CSVs in S3","SageMaker Feature Store","A notebook's local disk","CloudWatch Logs"],"q":"Two teams need the same features with point-in-time correctness for training and low-latency online serving. Where should the features live?","why":"Feature Store serves consistent online and offline features; raw S3 cannot guarantee point-in-time correctness."}],"resources":[{"label":"ihatesea69 labs and questions","note":"Topic-grouped practice questions for the drill blocks.","url":"https://github.com/ihatesea69/aws-mla-study-notes-and-hands-on-labs"},{"label":"bli55 notes","note":"Re-read source for reds that need depth.","url":"https://github.com/bli55/notes-aws-machine-learning"},{"label":"ccsusan notes","note":"Quick-reference tables for fast re-reads.","url":"https://github.com/ccsusan/aws-mla-certification-notes"}],"title":"Weak-Area Repair Day","week":6},{"blocks":[{"label":"Exam simulation","steps":["Block 170 uninterrupted minutes: phone away, no notes, no pausing, no second browser tab.","Take Tutorials Dojo MLA-C02 practice exam #1 (85 questions) — search for the Tutorials Dojo MLA-C02 practice exams if you do not have the link saved.","Use flag-and-return: answer each question in about 2 minutes, flag the uncertain ones, return with remaining time.","Record your total score and the per-domain breakdown."],"time":"3h"},{"label":"Rigorous review","steps":["For every wrong answer, write two things: why the right answer is right, and why your pick was wrong.","Apply the same treatment to flagged questions you guessed right — luck is not knowledge.","Sort every miss into one bucket: knowledge gap, misread question, AWS-specific gotcha, or trap wording."],"time":"1.5h"},{"label":"Fix list","steps":["Turn each miss into a one-line rule on paper — this becomes your Day 29 and Day 30 review sheet.","Compare domain scores against the 28/24/24/24 weights: where did points actually leak?","Decide the 3 topics that get final repair on Day 29."],"time":"0.5h"}],"day":28,"domain":"All domains (exam simulation)","isCheckpoint":true,"isReview":false,"keyTerms":[{"def":"Exam wording that usually selects the cheapest managed option.","term":"Most cost-effective"},{"def":"Exam wording that selects the most managed service that fits.","term":"Least operational overhead"},{"def":"Points at KMS encryption, VPC controls, or IAM — read the scenario for which.","term":"Securely (exam signal)"},{"def":"Points at EventBridge, SageMaker Pipelines, or Model Monitor.","term":"Automatically (exam signal)"},{"def":"Answer in ~2 minutes, flag the uncertain, return with remaining time.","term":"Flag-and-return"}],"notes":[{"heading":"Exam conditions that matter","points":["Timed, no notes, no pausing — practice the exact discomfort the real exam produces.","Train flag-and-return now: the first pass banks easy points; the second pass spends time where it counts.","Never leave a question blank; there is no penalty for guessing on this exam."]},{"heading":"The review protocol","points":["Every miss gets: the right answer's reasoning plus the cause of your error.","Categorize: knowledge gap (re-study), misread (slow down), AWS gotcha (flashcard), trap wording (log the pattern).","Repeat misses across exams are emergencies — they signal a systemic misunderstanding, not bad luck."]},{"heading":"Reading the score","points":["Compare per-domain scores against the 28/24/24/24 weights, not just the total.","A low Domain 1 score costs more than a low Domain 4 score — weight your repairs accordingly.","Distinguish knowledge gaps from misreads: only the first needs study time; the second needs discipline."]},{"heading":"Trap patterns to log","points":["`Most cost-effective` usually means the cheapest managed option, not the cleverest architecture.","`Least operational overhead` means the most managed service that fits.","`Securely` points at KMS, VPC controls, or IAM — read which one the scenario needs.","`Automatically` points at EventBridge, Pipelines, or Model Monitor — the hands-off answer."]}],"objectives":["Sit a full 85-question, 170-minute timed simulation under exam conditions","Score it and categorize every miss by domain and cause","Turn every miss into a one-line rule on a fix list"],"quiz":[{"answer":1,"options":["A real-time endpoint running 24/7","SageMaker Batch Transform","Serverless inference","A larger real-time instance"],"q":"A team scores 10 million records once nightly from S3. Which is most cost-effective?","why":"Batch Transform runs only for the job — no idle endpoint billing for bulk offline work."},{"answer":1,"options":["Self-hosted RAG on EC2 with OpenSearch","Bedrock Knowledge Bases","Fine-tuning a model on SageMaker","Training a model from scratch"],"q":"A company wants a chatbot grounded in internal documents with the least operational overhead. Which fits?","why":"Least operational overhead selects the managed option — Knowledge Bases handles ingestion, retrieval, and grounding."},{"answer":1,"options":["Over-provision a real-time endpoint","SageMaker Serverless Inference","Batch Transform","A bigger instance type"],"q":"An endpoint has spiky, unpredictable traffic and long idle periods. How do you minimize idle cost?","why":"Serverless inference scales to zero — you pay per invocation, not for idle capacity."},{"answer":1,"options":["SSE-S3 only","SSE-KMS plus a bucket policy denying non-TLS access","Client-side encryption with no key management","Default S3 settings"],"q":"Training data must be encrypted with company-managed keys, and all S3 access must use HTTPS. Which combination?","why":"SSE-KMS gives key control; the aws:SecureTransport condition enforces TLS."},{"answer":1,"options":["Manual retraining each quarter","Model Monitor plus EventBridge plus SageMaker Pipelines plus a registry approval gate","A larger training instance","Deleting and recreating the endpoint weekly"],"q":"The team wants retraining to start on its own whenever drift is detected, with a human approving deployment. Which design?","why":"Monitor detects, EventBridge routes, Pipelines retrain, and the approval gate keeps the human checkpoint."}],"resources":[{"label":"MLA-C02 exam guide (PDF)","note":"Re-check any task statement your misses cluster around.","url":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/pdfs/aws-certification/latest/machine-learning-engineer-associate-02/machine-learning-engineer-associate-02.pdf"},{"label":"ccsusan cheat sheet","note":"Fast lookup while writing one-line rules for each miss.","url":"https://github.com/ccsusan/aws-mla-certification-notes"},{"label":"AWS Skill Builder","note":"Official practice material if you want a second question source.","url":"https://skillbuilder.aws"}],"title":"Timed Practice Exam #1 + Review","week":6},{"blocks":[{"label":"Exam simulation","steps":["Same conditions as Day 28: 170 minutes, no notes, no pausing, phone away.","Take Tutorials Dojo MLA-C02 practice exam #2 — a different question set from Day 28.","Flag-and-return discipline again: 2 minutes per question on the first pass.","Record total score and per-domain breakdown for comparison."],"time":"3h"},{"label":"Review + compare","steps":["Run the same rigorous review protocol: every miss gets the right-answer reasoning plus your error's cause.","Compare against Day 28: score delta, per-domain deltas, and repeat misses — repeats are the emergency.","Verify the Day 28 fix list: are those topics actually repaired, or did they miss again?"],"time":"1.5h"},{"label":"Readiness call","steps":["Scores consistent in your target band across both exams with margin to spare: you are ready.","Any domain still leaking heavily: that becomes Day 30 morning's only job — max 5 items, tactics only.","Write tomorrow's light-review list now, while the misses are fresh."],"time":"0.5h"}],"day":29,"domain":"All domains (exam simulation)","isCheckpoint":true,"isReview":false,"keyTerms":[{"def":"A topic missed on both practice exams — a systemic gap needing targeted repair.","term":"Repeat miss"},{"def":"Score split across the four domains, compared against the 28/24/24/24 weights.","term":"Per-domain breakdown"},{"def":"The score range you need with margin to spare, held across both simulations.","term":"Target band"},{"def":"One-line rules from every miss; the only study material for Day 30.","term":"Fix list"},{"def":"The readiness decision: consistent band plus no collapsing domain means go.","term":"Go/no-go call"}],"notes":[{"heading":"Comparing two exams","points":["The delta matters more than either absolute score — improvement should show in repaired topics.","Repeat misses across both exams signal systemic gaps; a third exam will not fix them, targeted repair will.","A stable per-domain profile beats a higher but volatile total."]},{"heading":"The readiness bar","points":["Consistent scores with margin above your target band across both simulations.","No single domain collapsing — remember Domain 1 carries 28%.","Flag-and-return feels automatic, not effortful."]},{"heading":"What Day 29 is not","points":["Not a cram day: no new topics, no new services, no new notes documents.","Not a third exam: two clean simulations plus review beats three rushed ones.","The remaining work is recognition and tactics, not acquisition."]}],"objectives":["Sit a second full timed simulation; compare score and miss patterns against Day 28","Confirm the weak areas from Day 28 are repaired","Make the go/no-go call on exam readiness"],"quiz":[{"answer":1,"options":["Build a custom review app on EC2","Augmented AI (A2I)","SageMaker Ground Truth","A larger model"],"q":"Low-confidence predictions need human verification before the business acts on them, with the least implementation effort. Which fits?","why":"Least effort plus human review of live predictions is exactly A2I's purpose."},{"answer":1,"options":["Provisioned real-time endpoint","SageMaker Serverless Inference","Batch Transform","Multi-model endpoint"],"q":"Inference traffic is occasional and unpredictable; idle cost must be near zero. Which option?","why":"Serverless inference scales to zero and bills per invocation."},{"answer":1,"options":["Model Monitor","SageMaker Clarify","A2I","CloudTrail"],"q":"The team must check training data for bias before training starts. Which service?","why":"Clarify computes pre-training bias metrics; Model Monitor watches for bias drift after deployment."},{"answer":1,"options":["CSV files in S3","SageMaker Feature Store","Notebook local disk","A wiki page"],"q":"Features must be shared across teams with point-in-time correctness for training and low-latency online serving. Where do they live?","why":"Feature Store is the managed answer for consistent online/offline features."},{"answer":1,"options":["Data quality","Model quality","Both equally","Neither"],"q":"Which monitoring type needs human-verified ground-truth labels: data quality or model quality?","why":"Model quality metrics compare predictions against true labels; data quality compares against the baseline alone."}],"resources":[{"label":"MLA-C02 exam guide (PDF)","note":"Final check on any task statement both exams probed.","url":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/pdfs/aws-certification/latest/machine-learning-engineer-associate-02/machine-learning-engineer-associate-02.pdf"},{"label":"ccsusan cheat sheet","note":"Recognition-only review for Day 30's light list.","url":"https://github.com/ccsusan/aws-mla-certification-notes"},{"label":"AWS Skill Builder","note":"Official material if a repeat miss needs one more authoritative source.","url":"https://skillbuilder.aws"}],"title":"Timed Practice Exam #2 + Readiness Check","week":6},{"blocks":[{"label":"Tactics lock-in","steps":["Pacing math: 170 minutes over 85 questions is about 2 minutes per question; set checkpoints (for example, question 21 by ~40 minutes, question 43 by ~85).","Flag-and-return: never spend more than 3 minutes on one question in the first pass.","Heuristics: most cost-effective means cheapest managed option; least overhead means most managed; securely points at KMS, VPC, or IAM; automatically points at EventBridge or Pipelines.","Read every NOT and EXCEPT twice — they invert the question."],"time":"1h"},{"label":"Logistics checklist","steps":["Confirm the booking: date, time, and test-center address — or run the online-proctoring system check now, not tomorrow.","ID ready: government-issued, name matching the registration exactly.","Test center: arrive 30 minutes early; know what is allowed in the room. Online: quiet room, clear desk, working webcam and mic, stable internet.","Lay out everything tonight and set two alarms."],"time":"1h"},{"label":"Light review","steps":["Read your one-line fix lists from Days 28–29 — recognition only, no new study.","Take the 3 confidence questions below; if all three feel obvious, you are done.","Close the books. The work is banked."],"time":"0.5h"},{"label":"Rest","steps":["Stop studying. Walk, eat well, sleep early.","Do not open new material tonight — cramming now only adds noise.","Trust the six weeks: you built this knowledge deliberately, and it is yours."],"time":"evening"}],"day":30,"domain":"All domains (exam readiness)","isCheckpoint":false,"isReview":false,"keyTerms":[{"def":"First pass in ~2 min/question, flag the uncertain, spend remaining time on flags.","term":"Flag-and-return"},{"def":"Time check at fixed question counts (21/43/64) to stay on the 170-minute budget.","term":"Pacing checkpoint"},{"def":"85 questions, 170 minutes; results typically arrive within about five business days.","term":"Beta exam format"}],"notes":[{"heading":"Pacing math","points":["85 questions, 170 minutes: about 2 minutes per question on average.","Checkpoints keep you honest: question 21 by ~40 minutes, 43 by ~85, 64 by ~128.","Behind at a checkpoint: speed up the first pass and lean harder on flag-and-return.","Ahead at a checkpoint: spend the surplus on flagged questions, not on second-guessing green ones."]},{"heading":"The four heuristics","points":["`Most cost-effective`: the cheapest managed option that meets the requirement.","`Least operational overhead` / `least effort`: the most managed service that fits.","`Securely`: KMS encryption, VPC controls, or IAM — the scenario tells you which.","`Automatically`: EventBridge, SageMaker Pipelines, or Model Monitor — the hands-off answer."]},{"heading":"Question-attack basics","points":["Read the last sentence first — it tells you what is actually being asked.","Eliminate the obviously wrong two, then decide between the survivors.","Watch for NOT and EXCEPT: they invert the question and the exam uses them deliberately.","No penalty for guessing — never leave a question blank."]},{"heading":"Tonight","points":["No new material. Recognition-only review of your own fix lists, then stop.","Logistics beat studying now: ID, alarms, route or proctoring setup.","Sleep is the highest-value activity left on the schedule."]}],"objectives":["Lock in pacing and question-attack tactics","Confirm every logistics item the night before","Rest — no new material today"],"quiz":[{"answer":1,"options":["Keep working — you are close","Flag it, move on, return with remaining time","Guess randomly and never return","Skip it permanently"],"q":"You have 170 minutes for 85 questions. One question has eaten 4 minutes and you are stuck. What do you do?","why":"Flag-and-return protects the easy points; sunk time on one question is the classic pacing failure."},{"answer":1,"options":["Train the custom model — more control","Bedrock Knowledge Bases","Both are equally managed","Neither; build on EC2"],"q":"Two answers both look right: train a custom model on SageMaker, or use Bedrock Knowledge Bases. The question says with the least operational overhead. Which do you pick?","why":"Least operational overhead always selects the most managed option that fits."},{"answer":1,"options":["Learning two new AWS services","Light review of your fix lists, logistics check, then sleep","A third full practice exam","Rewriting all your notes"],"q":"The night before the exam, the highest-value activity is…","why":"Recognition plus rest beats acquisition the night before — the work is already banked."}],"resources":[{"label":"MLA-C02 exam guide (web)","note":"One last skim of the domain weights — then close it.","url":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/aws-certification/latest/machine-learning-engineer-associate-02/machine-learning-engineer-associate-02.html"},{"label":"AWS Skill Builder","note":"Only if a fix-list item needs one final authoritative check.","url":"https://skillbuilder.aws"}],"title":"Exam-Day Tactics, Logistics & Rest","week":6});